{"y":2013,"label":"2012–13","coach":"Kevin Ollie","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":20,"l":10,"cw":10,"cl":8,"confFinish":"7th in Big East","finish":"none","apHigh":21,"srs":11.89,"sos":7.42,"ortg":103.8,"drtg":97.2,"ppg":69.9,"oppg":65.4,"story":{"headline":"Ollie's First Year: No Postseason, No Surrender","text":"Kevin Ollie, a Calhoun-era point guard and 13-year NBA journeyman, took over a team that was banned from the postseason because of a low Academic Progress Rate. His debut came on November 9, 2012, at Ramstein Air Base in Germany, where UConn beat No. 14 Michigan State 66–62 in the Armed Forces Classic. At the Paradise Jam in St. Thomas, UConn beat Wake Forest and in-state Quinnipiac in double overtime before New Mexico won the final 66–60. Shabazz Napier (first-team All-Big East) and Ryan Boatright (15.4 points) formed one of the best backcourts in the country, the Huskies won at No. 17 Notre Dame, and they finished 20–10 and 10–8 in what turned out to be the final season of the original Big East. Ollie received the Ben Jobe Award, and UConn stayed behind in the conference's successor, the American Athletic Conference.","moments":[{"date":"2012-11-09","title":"Ollie's debut in Germany","text":"UConn beats No. 14 Michigan State 66–62 at Ramstein Air Base in Kevin Ollie's first game."}],"honors":["Armed Forces Classic champions","Kevin Ollie – Ben Jobe Award","Shabazz Napier – First-team All-Big East"],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012%E2%80%9313_Connecticut_Huskies_men's_basketball_team","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UConn_Huskies_men%27s_basketball","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Ollie","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shabazz_Napier","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryan_Boatright"]},"roster":[{"pid":"shabazz-napier-1","name":"Shabazz Napier","num":"13","cls":"JR","pos":"G","ht":"6-1","wt":180,"home":"Roxbury, MA","hs":"Charlestown","rsci":"75 ( 2010 )","photo":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/2025-01-0